            <ArticleTitle>[Prevalence and aetiopathogenesis of neuropathic pain in elderly cancer patients].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>159-63</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.regg.2015.07.008</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0211-139X(15)00135-3</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The prevalence of neuropathic pain is difficult to estimate as most studies evaluating chronic pain do not differentiate neuropathic from nociceptive pain. There are only a few studies of neuropathic pain in the elderly, specifically in the oncology population. This article is a non-systematic review of the relevant evidence on the prevalence and aetiopathogenesis of neuropathic cancer pain in the elderly.</AbstractText>
